DA  - 2022
AB  - Penile squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) is rare, with limited treatment options in advanced disease. We report a 52-year-old male with recurrent/metastatic penile SCC, high PD-L1 expression (90%) and TMB (14.4 muts/Mb), who progressed despite penectomy, lymph node dissection, chemoradiotherapy and chemotherapy. He achieved progression-free survival exceeding 32 months with continuous sintilimab, though developed ICI-induced diabetes after 24 cycles requiring insulin, despite negative type 1 diabetes autoantibodies but susceptible HLA genotype DR3-DQ2. This is the first reported case of a radiation- and multichemorefractory penile SCC patient obtaining a remarkable, durable (>32 months) anti-tumor response to continuous sintilimab, complicated by immune-related diabetes.
